+
+/// SweetMetal2 (u-t): Turnip's timelines are Mesa's emulated ones (one kernel fence per point), whose wait for any of several
+/// (`vk_sync.c`, `__vk_sync_wait_many`: "nothing better we can do than spin") polls every semaphore with no sleep until one
+/// lands or the slice (100 ms) runs out: the scheduler's thread spun a core in kernel fence calls whenever its heads waited
+/// for GPU signals. So the thread sleeps on its condition variable there, woken by the completion turns, as on a driver with
+/// `WaitForAnyObjectLosesASignal`, unless `SWEET_SCHED_GPU_WAIT=1`.
+fn sweet_gpu_wait() -> bool {
+    std::env::var("SWEET_SCHED_GPU_WAIT").is_ok_and(|v| v.trim() == "1")
 }
 
